% ============================================================================
% chamber-branch.query.adj — worked recall over the civics
% chamber/branch two-hop composition library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "which branch is the
% Senate part of?" — it states no civics fact of its own — it only
% `import`s the grounded rule and asks binding queries. The answer is
% DERIVED, composing congress-chamber's chamber->body fact with
% government-branch-member's branch->member fact through the `congress`
% atom they share, so every answer carries BOTH citations in its `steps`
% trail. The last two queries demonstrate INHERITED abstention: the rule
% adds no facts of its own, so it abstains exactly where its premises do.
%
% Run (from this directory so the relative imports resolve):
%     ../../../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli chamber-branch.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "chamber-branch.adj"

? chamber_branch(senate, $B)                   % expect legislative (direct, two-hop derivation)
? chamber_branch(house_of_representatives, $B) % expect legislative (direct)
? chamber_branch($C, legislative)              % expect two solutions: senate, house_of_representatives (reverse -- enumerates a branch's chambers)
? chamber_branch(supreme_court, $B)            % expect abstain -- congress_chamber abstains on it (judicial branch, not a chamber of Congress), so this rule inherits that abstention
? chamber_branch(president, $B)                % expect abstain -- likewise executive
